WEBVTT


00:00:00.988 --> 00:00:06.988
Hello, welcome back to our course. Today we are going to continue talking about graphics in R.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:12.512
So far, we have seen a few functions which were well handed to create plots in base R.

00:00:12.551 --> 00:00:21.551
But now, we would like to see other functions, from a package called ggplot2. Ggplot2 is based on the book "grammar of graphics",

00:00:21.095 --> 00:00:33.095
by Leland Wilkinson. The package uses a high-level graphic syntax that allows us to stack different levels of data visualization, and you will see, that it allows us to create really fancy-looking plots.

00:00:33.553 --> 00:00:44.553
There are many places to get help with gtplot2: RStudio community, Stack overflow or like chat boxes. Or you can basically use a browser of your choice and look for some additional info.

00:00:45.537 --> 00:00:56.537
Okay, so all ggplot2 plots start with a call to a ggplot function. Then in that ggplot function, we first set the default data

00:00:59.294 --> 00:01:02.294
and the aesthetic mappings with aes(). And then we add layers (geomes).

00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:08.303
We can add geom_point(), geom_abline(), geom_histogram(), we are going to see the examples of them. Scales:

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:14.375
so basically, something about labels, axes or titles.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:20.616
And then facets. Basically, here we define a theme, and then we add all of that through a

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:26.409
plus sign. Okay, now let us have a look at the first example. So in the first example,

00:01:27.129 --> 00:01:41.129
after installing and loading ggplot2, we are going to look into the data frame called "mtcars" and then we look at what it contains. Well, it contains different makes of cars. Then,

00:01:47.811 --> 00:01:50.811
as variables, we have characteristics of these cars. Now I would like to create a scatter plot. How can I do that using ggplot2 functions? Well,

00:01:57.069 --> 00:02:02.069
I call ggplot, then I insert my data. Then I select aesthetics, so x is wt, y is mpg and then after the plus sign, I

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:08.688
would like to now create a scatter plot. So I'm calling geom_point. Okay, what can I see? I can see that, indeed, I have

00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:17.047
wt on the x-axis, I have mpg on the y-axis, and then I have points. Indeed, I created a scatterplot.

00:02:18.109 --> 00:02:25.109
Now I would like to adjust my scatter plot. For example, by changing the size and the shape. Basically, the first line is

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:30.689
almost exactly the same and the second line, I would like to

00:02:30.774 --> 00:02:35.774
enlarge the size and change the shape. And you can see, that the plot

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:45.069
looks already much better. I could even make a fancier plot. For example, I could make a plot in which the points would

00:02:45.692 --> 00:02:53.692
match different levels of another variable in my dataset. So, for example, here we have this cyl

00:03:00.335 --> 00:03:05.335
variable and it's numeric. But it has only 4 values, 6, 4, 8. So we could coerce it to be a factor variable using as.factor() function.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:10.317
And now, let us have a look at our plot. Once again, I'm passing

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:17.823
my data, and I'm selecting aesthetics. These would be the same, x, wt, y, mpg, and now I would like to have

00:03:21.113 --> 00:03:29.113
points, which match different levels of that factor variable. So first, in geom_point(), I'm defining a size, I would like to have decently sized

00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:36.422
points. Then I would like my color matching different levels of my

00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:43.756
factor variable cyl. And I would like to have shapes matching different levels of my variable cyl.

00:03:44.673 --> 00:03:48.673
Now, I would also like to add labels to my scatter plot.

00:03:51.376 --> 00:03:55.376
So that can be done, using geom_text(). How can I do it? I pass to aes()

00:03:55.427 --> 00:04:01.427
parameter label and now my labels would be the row names of the mtcars

00:04:01.857 --> 00:04:09.857
data frame. And now, on top of that, I would also add linear regression lines. So basically here, I could do it through geom_smooth.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.630
Then in aes, I would like to have three lines,

00:04:20.741 --> 00:04:24.741
corresponding to 3 different levels of my cyl factor viable. I'm selecting a method to be a linear model, because I would like to fit this linear regression.

00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:31.983
And I wouldn't like to have any error bars around my linear regression, so I'm setting SE to false.

00:04:32.812 --> 00:04:38.812
And I would like to have these lines on a full range of x. Not only on those x's,

00:04:39.786 --> 00:04:44.786
which correspond to a particular level of this factor variable. Okay, what can we see?

00:04:45.374 --> 00:04:50.374
We can see, indeed, that our plot looks good. We have different colors

00:04:51.327 --> 00:05:00.327
for different levels of our cyl covariant. We have different shapes, we also have regression lines and indeed, we have different makes of cars for each dot.

00:05:01.096 --> 00:05:17.096
Okay, now we could also create a bar plot. So a bar plot here, could be once again created by calling ggplot functions. So first I'm selecting my data. I would like to create a data frame. In that data frame, I'm having two

00:05:17.478 --> 00:05:22.478
variables. The first one is this x variable, with only 3 different levels, repeated twice.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:34.710
And the second one is the y variable with different numeric values. Then I'm passing my data to ggplot function. I'm here, setting the aesthetics, so x is

00:05:35.833 --> 00:05:46.833
corresponding to my x variable, y is corresponding to my y variable. And then in my bar plot, what am I doing here? I'm calling geom_bar layer, and then in aesthetics, I'm selecting this

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:01.389
fill parameter, and fill should match "A" "B" "C" to different fill colors. So I would like to have different fill colors corresponding to different levels of my x variable. Then I'm also setting stat to identity,

00:06:01.299 --> 00:06:12.299
because I would like to use y values as they are. Not summarize them or count them, etc. And then I'm also adjusting this theme to be a light

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.210
theme. Okay, let us have a look. Indeed, now I have a bar plot. We have different colors for different levels of x values.

00:06:22.231 --> 00:06:28.231
And then we have x on the x-axis, y on the y-axis And then, indeed, you can see that

00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:34.027
these bars now correspond to the sums of y's for a specific "A".

00:06:43.683 --> 00:06:47.683
And indeed, the plot already looks quite good. We could also look at box plots, so here, let us call our dataset "iris". You might remember, that in that dataset, we had

00:06:47.533 --> 00:06:56.533
different widths of sepal for different species of irises. So now I'm passing that dataset to my ggplot function.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.170
And in aesthetics for x, I am selecting "species", because I would like to have different plots for different species.

00:07:11.891 --> 00:07:13.891
And for y, I'm selecting "sepal width". And then, I'm selecting this fill parameter to be "species", because I would like to have different fills of box plots

00:07:14.017 --> 00:07:21.017
corresponding to different levels of my factor variable "species".

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:25.969
Okay, so now I'm adding a layer geom_boxplot, and then I could also adjust

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:36.919
the titles of my main title and of the title of x- and y-axis. So here, for example

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:42.917
I'm setting the main title to "Sepal Width by species". And then I'm setting x

00:07:42.993 --> 00:07:48.993
to be "species", and y-axis to be "sepal width".

00:07:58.175 --> 00:08:01.175
We could also style these titles. For example, I could set the color of the main title to be red and the face to be bold italic.

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:12.053
And then I could adjust the color of the x-axis to be blue, and of a different size and face. You could also adjust

00:08:12.672 --> 00:08:19.672
the way the legend looks like. So here, I would like to have it in light blue. As you can see, instead of having it on a default

00:08:19.452 --> 00:08:24.452
background. Okay, now we can see that my box plots already look

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:33.230
quite good. We have different colors for the titles of the x-axis, y-axis.

00:08:32.808 --> 00:08:39.808
We have a different colors for the main title and we indeed adjusted the background of our legend. Then you can see that fills

00:08:46.713 --> 00:08:48.713
match different levels of my species. And also the legend matches the colors in the plot.

00:08:49.534 --> 00:08:56.534
We could also create histograms. Let us create another dataset, mydata. In that myata, I would have two

00:08:59.212 --> 00:09:02.212
variables. The first variable is a factor variable, with two levels, "F" and "M",

00:09:02.435 --> 00:09:10.435
each repeated 200 times. The second variable is this vector, composed of two subvectors. These two subvectors

00:09:10.355 --> 00:09:19.355
are containing values from a normal distribution. So basically, each of them contains 200 values.

00:09:19.375 --> 00:09:24.375
Then they were sampled from a normal distribution, with a mean of 55

00:09:24.089 --> 00:09:33.089
and then with a mean of 65. I bundle them together and I round all the values not to have any digits.

00:09:44.479 --> 00:09:47.479
Now I pass that data set to my ggplot. Select my aesthetics, so x is white. "Color" now  corresponds to "sex", because I would like to have different colors for different sex values.

00:09:48.808 --> 00:09:53.808
Then, I would like to create a histogram, so I'm adding a layer geom_histogram.

00:09:52.853 --> 00:09:58.853
And then, in aesthetics, I would like to have that histogram with a density.

00:09:59.684 --> 00:10:02.684
Then I would like to have that histogram with bars,

00:10:02.412 --> 00:10:07.412
which are black. So the contouring would be black and the filling would be white.

00:10:07.048 --> 00:10:15.048
And then, I would like to also superimpose on these histograms a density function.

00:10:15.543 --> 00:10:25.543
I would like to fill that density function with that color "#FF6666" and I would like to have it semi-transparent. Basically that alpha parameter

00:10:25.504 --> 00:10:31.504
defines the transparency level. So "0" is

00:10:43.873 --> 00:10:51.873
for a fully transparent plot and "1" for a fully opaque one. Then I would like to have my legend at the top of the plot. Okay here we go. Indeed, very nicely looking histograms. For female and for male we have these two density plots. Indeed,

00:10:52.397 --> 00:10:59.397
they are semi-transparent. So through the density plots I can see my histograms.

00:10:59.801 --> 00:11:06.801
Alright, so now we could also talk about creating a snapshot of a three-dimensional plot in R.

00:11:17.625 --> 00:11:25.625
So this is handy for visualizing three-dimensional random variables. Let us install and call that package, "mvtnorm". Then I would like to define a grid of x and y values. These are defined through that seq() function.

00:11:31.221 --> 00:11:36.221
And now, I need to construct a function f. Which basically takes these two values and spits out

00:11:36.124 --> 00:11:44.124
a multivariate density function. That density corresponds to the normal distribution.

00:11:44.383 --> 00:11:51.383
Don't worry about the functions, just take it as given. You are going to understand the syntax later.

00:12:00.538 --> 00:12:05.538
Since this is a 3D plot, I need x-,y-axis and a z-axis. How do I compute that z? This z is basically that function f,

00:12:06.242 --> 00:12:09.242
applied to x and y. Then this is my z-

00:12:09.108 --> 00:12:14.108
axis. Once again, I would like to plot

00:12:14.858 --> 00:12:19.858
these. Here, I use the function persp(). I call

00:12:26.892 --> 00:12:28.892
persp() and there we have x, y and z. Then we have different parameters. We have this parameter theta,

00:12:36.349 --> 00:12:41.349
which corresponds to setting the rotation view around the z-axis. We have parameter phi, which tilts the camera from above towards the horizon. We have an expand parameter,

00:12:41.492 --> 00:12:49.492
that offers us vertical scaling of z. We have a color parameter, to set a color.

00:12:49.802 --> 00:12:58.802
And then we have a shade parameter, that is a shading strength. Let us have a look. Indeed, this is a two-dimensional snapshot of a three-dimensional picture.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.030
And it already looks quite good. We could adjust it, for example, by changing this theta parameter,

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:10.530
phi parameter, expand parameter, etc. Then you can see the snapshot

00:13:14.568 --> 00:13:17.568
from a slightly different angle. We could also

00:13:16.733 --> 00:13:22.733
plot contour plots. For example, if we have three-dimensional data, these contour plots are 2D

00:13:27.571 --> 00:13:32.571
visualizations of a three-dimensional plot, basically a map. An object from 3D to a plane in 2D. There is this orthogonal mapping.

00:13:32.296 --> 00:13:36.296
Then, here I need to call a function contour.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:44.520
I pass x, y and z. Then I select the number of levels, here it's 20. Indeed, you can see that control plot.

00:13:44.637 --> 00:13:53.637
Of course, we could adjust it, for example, we could change the number of levels, but we could also adjust the coloring. So here I would like to see these contours

00:13:52.934 --> 00:13:59.934
in rainbow colors. Okay, that's it for the graphics from my side. Thank you for your attention.

